# Doctrine Week 2. Rebuilt on the deck template from the old one-page lesson, section for section.
# fig cards are the gold cards of the old page, lit cards are the crimson ones (wrath).

=== Introduction
--- How God Reveals Himself to Man [title]
overline: Faith Baptist Church of Plymouth County · Adult Sunday School · Week 2
heading: How God Reveals Himself to Man
sub: General Revelation vs. Specific Revelation: how God speaks through nature, His actions, and His Word
big: Psalm 19:1-4
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move through the lesson. Tap any gold reference to read the verse. The Contents button lists every section.

=== I. General & Specific Revelation
--- General & Specific Revelation
lead: Have you ever looked at a sunset, or read a Bible verse, and felt God’s presence? God’s revelation comes in two forms: one available to all, one given through His Word.

--- General Revelation: Available to All
p: God revealing Himself through creation and the human conscience, His law written on men’s hearts. Universal, non-verbal: it points to God’s existence but not to the specifics of salvation. All creation is a parable for us to know God.
fig: Without Excuse | Romans 1:18-20
fig: The Heavens Declare His Glory | Psalm 19:1-4
fig: The Law Written on Their Hearts | Romans 2:14-15 | This internal moral compass, the conscience, is part of General Revelation.
callout: What about that guy on that deserted island? What about people who have never heard of Jesus?

--- Specific Revelation: Through the Word
p: God revealing Himself through direct means: His Word, the prophets, and Jesus Christ. Detailed and verbal, it reveals God’s plan for salvation, His commandments, and prophecy for times past and times to come. Specific Revelation builds on General Revelation, giving clarity so that we might know God’s will, collectively and individually.
fig: God Hath Spoken by His Son | Hebrews 1:1-2
fig: In the Beginning Was the Word | John 1:1 | Jesus is God’s ultimate revelation.
fig: Profitable for Doctrine | 2 Timothy 3:16-17

=== II. Revelation Through Creation
--- Revelation Through Creation
lead: Creation (sky, stars, nature, animals) declares God’s glory, power, and wisdom, leaving mankind without excuse.
fig: Day Unto Day Uttereth Speech | Psalm 19:1-3
fig: Clearly Seen, Understood by the Things Made | Romans 1:20
fig: Ask Now the Beasts | Job 12:7-9
p: A bird’s migration shows God’s design. The miracle of birth, the intricacy of DNA: all of nature is a parable pointing back to its Maker.

=== III. Revelation Through Wrath
--- Revelation Through Wrath
lead: God’s wrath is not arbitrary. It is a response to sin, revealing His holiness, His justice, and His moral standard.
lit: Angry with the Wicked Every Day | Psalm 7:11-13
lit: How Long, O Lord? | Revelation 6:10
lit: Vengeance Belongeth Unto Me | Hebrews 10:30

--- Wrath on the Nations
lit: The {Lord} Hath a Controversy with the Nations | Jeremiah 25:31-32
lit: Abhor That Which Is Evil | Romans 12:9
p: Seeing wrath fall on the wicked, even natural consequences of sin, teaches unbelievers that there is a just Creator. Even the secular idea of “karma” points to a moral order only a just God could establish. God judges nations, not only individuals, by their works.
callout: How does knowing God judges fairly affect our view of Him?
callout: Why is it important to teach about God’s wrath alongside His love?

=== IV. Revelation Through Mercy
--- Revelation Through Mercy
lead: Where wrath reveals God’s justice, mercy reveals His love: slow to anger, rich in compassion toward the undeserving.
fig: Merciful and Gracious, Slow to Anger | Psalm 103:8
fig: Rich in Mercy, Great Love | Ephesians 2:4
fig: That They Also May Obtain Mercy | Romans 11:30-31
p: When we show others the mercy and grace God has shown us, it reveals His nature to them. They hear our praise for answered prayer, or see a changed life, and come to a better understanding of His longsuffering, and He receives the glory for loving His people.
callout: Leader: share a brief story of God’s mercy, personal or biblical, such as the Prodigal Son.

=== V. Revelation Through the Word
--- Revelation Through the Word
lead: God spoke through the prophets in ages past; now He has spoken through His Son, and given us Scripture, breathed out by His Spirit.
fig: By Whom Also He Made the Worlds | Hebrews 1:1-2
fig: All Things Were Made by Him | John 1:1-3
fig: Moved by the Holy Ghost | 2 Peter 1:20-21 | Prophets were the pen, God the Author: the burden of the {Lord}, delivered faithfully.

--- Scripture Is God-Breathed
p: Scripture is “God-breathed,” written by men carried along by the Holy Ghost: the prophets were the pen, God was the Author. This is what secures Scripture’s trustworthiness as God’s Specific Revelation.
callout: Why is it comforting to know Scripture comes from God, and not just human ideas?
callout: How has a Bible verse, or a story about Jesus, helped you know God better?
